nt of patients' nutritional status. This group includes doctors and nutritionists who utilize tools like MUST to evaluate malnutrition risk, particularly in vulnerable populations such as those undergoing hematopoietic cell transplants. Their involvement is essential for ensuring that patients receive appropriate nutritional interventions, which can significantly impact recovery and overall health outcomes. The integration of these professionals within the Sistema Nacional de Salud highlights the importance of coordinated care in addressing malnutrition [Data: Entities (13775); Relationships (1902, 3911, 3975, +more)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Influence of organizations like ESPEN and BAPEN\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Organizations such as the European Society of Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (ESPEN) and the British Association of Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (BAPEN) are instrumental in advancing nutritional standards and practices. ESPEN's guidelines on clinical nutrition and BAPEN's development of the MUST tool reflect their commitment to improving nutritional care across Europe. These organizations provide essential resources and recommendations that inform healthcare professionals on best practices, thereby enhancing the quality of care for patients requiring nutritional support [Data: Entities (1634, 8017); Relationships (2927, 3904, 3907, +more)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Diverse nutritional assessment tools in use\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The community encompasses various nutritional assessment tools beyond MUST, such as the Nutritional Risk Screening (NRS2002) and the Rapid Screen. These tools are designed to identify nutritional risk in different populations, including the elderly and oncology patients. The use of multiple screening methods allows for a comprehensive approach to assessing nutritional status, ensuring that healthcare providers can tailor interventions to meet the